<h3>Dr. Normand Theriault</h3>
<p>Adjunct Instructor, Philosophy &amp; Humanities — Houston City College</p>
<p>Welcome to Introduction to Philosophy. My name is Normand Thériault, and I’ll be your instructor this semester. I’ve been teaching philosophy at HCC for a number of years, and my enthusiasm for the subject hasn’t dimmed — if anything, it’s grown. I hope some of that comes through in the pages ahead.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Philosophy, as we’ll practice it in this course, isn’t about memorizing what famous thinkers believed. It’s about following arguments carefully, noticing when something doesn’t quite add up, and being willing to sit with a hard question long enough to actually think it through. That takes patience — but it’s also, I think, one of the more genuinely useful things a person can learn to do.</p>
<p>My own interests in philosophy began long before I started teaching it. Over the years I’ve found that the philosophers we’ll be reading — Socrates, Descartes, Hume, Hegel, Marx, Sartre — keep repaying attention. They tend to ask questions we thought we’d already answered.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Questions? Get in touch.</p>
<p>The best way to reach me is through the <strong>Canvas Inbox</strong>. I check it regularly and aim to respond within 48 hours on weekdays. If you have a question that might benefit your classmates, consider posting it to the <strong>Student Lounge</strong> discussion board instead.</p>
</div></div>
